title: Psalm 51: Have Mercy upon Me, O God
description: Psalm 51, David's cry of contrition when Nathan the prophet came unto him: confession, the false doctrine of original sin guilt, the washing of forgiveness, the joy of salvation restored, and the broken and contrite heart God will not despise, in the King James Bible.
output: lessons/bible/psalm-51.html
context: Psalm 51

# Built from the Psalm 51 series study (studies/data/books/Psalms.json -> studies -> "51" -> main, posts 18011,
# 18012, 18013, 18021 and 18022, 23-25 October 2026), in full and in its own order.
# Layout (as in the Psalms of David's Flight decks): the psalm is not read in full up front. The study goes paragraph
# by paragraph in its own wording, one to three paragraphs to a slide. The psalm verse a paragraph speaks of comes
# first on its slide, as big: (scripture: for two verses or more), then the paragraph. Every verse of the psalm is
# read once; a verse the study does not quote sits with the point nearest it (v. 10 with the new spirit, vv. 14-15
# with singing aloud of His righteousness, vv. 18-19 with the closing paragraph). Every passage the study quotes
# (2 Samuel 12, 1 John, Isaiah, Romans, Galatians, Luke) stays where the study has it, after the sentence that
# introduces it. Verses the study typed out are shown from the KJV by the builder.
# Fixed: "the transgression towards God that out him in judgment" -> "put him in judgment"; "the very account being
# mentioned show" -> "shows"; "in comparison and restitution of their relationship" -> "in compassion and
# restitution"; "Our flesh sins. If we deny that we are liars." -> "If we deny that, we are liars." Nothing else.
# The same fixes are made in the study itself (review/corrections.json), with two of its typed verses put back to the
# KJV: "thy holy Spirit" -> "thy holy spirit" (51:11) and "the joy of thy salvation and uphold" -> "salvation; and" (51:12).
# context: Psalm 51 gives the Chapter panel (key C): the whole chapter of each slide's passage, Psalm 51 always a tab.

=== Introduction
--- Have Mercy upon Me, O God [title]
overline: A Psalm of David
heading: Psalm 51: Have Mercy upon Me, O God
sub: Confession • Mercy • Forgiveness • The Joy of Salvation • A Contrite Heart
refs: Psalm 51:1; 2 Samuel 12:7; 1 John 1:9; Psalm 51:17
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any reference to read the verse.

=== David at his lowest
--- The clearest cry of contrition
kicker: Psalm 51 · David at his lowest
p: **Psalm 51** is the clearest and most sober cry of contrition and supplication for forgiveness by a believer, in the face of a judgment by the Almighty God, that we have in Scripture.
p: It is David at his lowest. "A Psalm of David, when Nathan the prophet came unto him, after he had gone in to Bath-sheba."

--- We know the story
kicker: 2 Samuel 11 · Bathsheba
p: We know the story. David doesn't go off to war as kings were expected to. He stays at his place and sees Bathsheba, the wife of his soldier, Uriah the Hittite, bathing on her nearby roof. Filled with lust, he has her. And as a consequence of that adultery, David conspired to have Uriah killed in battle, and wed his pregnant widow.

--- Thou art the man
kicker: 2 Samuel 12 · Nathan
p: Nathan confronts the King, and the Lord curses David for his sin;
scripture: 2 Samuel 12:7-10 | mark: Thou art the man

--- The child shall surely die
kicker: 2 Samuel 12 · Nathan
p: The judgment of God was given, the child should die.
scripture: 2 Samuel 12:14-15 | mark: the child also that is born unto thee shall surely die

--- Is the child dead?
kicker: 2 Samuel 12 · Nathan
p: David fasts and prays and stays by the child's side, but in the end the illness takes the boy.
scripture: 2 Samuel 12:18-19
p: David, devastated, cleans himself and goes into the house of the Lord to worship. It is in this context that this Psalm was written.

=== Have mercy upon me
--- According to thy lovingkindness
kicker: Psalm 51 · Have mercy upon me
big: Psalm 51:1 | mark: according to thy lovingkindness; the multitude of thy tender mercies
p: Rather than a root of bitterness against the judgement of God, as many are tempted to take on in the face of such fierce judgement, David concedes and comes to the Lord, asking for mercy. As he has so often in **Psalms**, he begins in worship, praising God for His lovingkindness and mercy. Reminding himself, and the Lord of His great qualities, and appeals to the ultimate judge to blot out his transgressions.

--- A formula of prayer
kicker: Application • A formula of prayer
p: This is a formula of prayer that we should commit to our prayer life. Under better circumstances and joyous events, or in grave repentance and regret for whatever sins we have committed, the Lord should be approached in humility and meekness of spirit. Hallowing His holy name.

--- Faithful to forgive
kicker: Psalm 51 · Wash me throughly
big: Psalm 51:2
p: The request is to forgive the confessed transgressions, knowing He is merciful and faithful to forgive His children.
big: 1 John 1:9 | mark: he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins

--- Even the sins of the level of David
kicker: Psalm 51 · Wash me throughly
p: This same promise applies to believers, today. The sure knowledge that our heavenly Father is faithful to forgive us, even the sins of the level of David. To wash us throughly, to cleanse us. To blot the iniquity from our record, and heal us, and our relationship with God.

--- If we confess our sins
kicker: Psalm 51 · I acknowledge my transgressions
big: Psalm 51:3 | mark: I acknowledge my transgressions
p: But note, it is if we confess our sins. David acknowledged his transgressions. We cannot lie to God and we should not lie to ourselves. We cannot hide from our sin when we've faced judgment, and our conscience keeps the sin ever before us in guilt.
p: The joy and benefit of the forgiveness of God comes in relief from that burden, and removing the fear of chastening, that comes from a contrite heart seeking His mercy.

=== Against thee, thee only
--- That thou mightest be justified
kicker: Psalm 51 · Against thee, thee only
big: Psalm 51:4 | mark: Against thee, thee only, have I sinned; that thou mightest be justified when thou speakest
p: David confesses that it is the transgression towards God that put him in judgment, and so he admits to God that not only has he sinned against Him, but that David acknowledges his understanding that God's judgments are right and deserved.

--- The better way
kicker: Application • Bitterness or confession
p: Again, it is very common to see people in sin, when facing the judgment of God for it, to grow to resent Him and despise His judgements. To make excuses for their acts, to deny their intent, to place what has resulted from their wicked ways on the shoulders of a God whom they reject as judge.
p: These people are destroyed with their bitterness, wracked with guilt, have a broken relationship with the God that bought them, and are often called home early, the result of their being incapable of being honest and appealing to His mercy.
p: But David shows us the better way.

=== Shapen in iniquity
--- A sinner, born of a sinner
kicker: Psalm 51 · Shapen in iniquity
p: David continues his confession;
big: Psalm 51:5
p: He uses hyperbolic language to say that he is a sinner, born of a sinner, in his appeal for mercy.
p: There is much made of this passage, and all manner of doctrines that have been spun out of it. The primary being "original sin" as the idea that babies are born guilty, and condemned.

--- I shall go to him
kicker: 2 Samuel 12 · The child
p: But the very account being mentioned shows that David did not believe any such thing.
scripture: 2 Samuel 12:21-23 | mark: I shall go to him, but he shall not return to me

--- Scripture with scripture
kicker: 2 Samuel 12 · The child
p: David, who knew he was redeemed by God, saved by faith, under the grace of the Lord, states plainly that he will go to be with his dead child in the end. That Heaven is their home.
p: While Psalms are dripping with doctrine, we should still always compare scripture with scripture, and gain understanding by the totality of the Bible, rather than out of context proof texts.

=== Truth in the inward parts
--- The honest confession of a righteous man
kicker: Psalm 51 · Truth in the inward parts
p: Continuing in **Psalm 51**, having established the context and getting the contention on the false doctrine of original sin guilt that comes from a shallow reading of the text, we see the purpose of the Psalm is the honest confession of a righteous man.
p: David is not placing the blame of what he has done on anyone else, and accepts full responsibility for whatever God judges against him. His fear of the Lord has led him to Godly repentance and reconciliation.

--- Thou desirest truth
kicker: Psalm 51 · Truth in the inward parts
big: Psalm 51:6 | mark: thou desirest truth in the inward parts
p: This is the meekness and honesty about our sin that God seeks, that in it, we can actually learn from Him and grow.

--- Not sacrifices and burnt offerings
kicker: Psalm 51 · Thou desirest not sacrifice
p: We discussed in the previous [[lesson:psalm-50|Psalm 50]] study that God doesn't seek the sacrifices and burnt offerings, but a meek and contrite heart. He is pleased as a Father when we come to Him for restitution and forgiveness. His mercy and grace are limitless, all He asks for is honesty in our heart.
big: Psalm 51:16 | mark: thou desirest not sacrifice

=== Purge me with hyssop
--- Whiter than snow
kicker: Psalm 51 · Purge me with hyssop
big: Psalm 51:7 | mark: Purge me with hyssop; whiter than snow
p: David confesses, and calls upon the Lord to purge him with hyssop, the sponge like plant that was used to apply the blood on the posts during the first passover, the same that was present when the Lord was crucified and they gave Him gall to drink.

--- White as snow
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:7
p: The washing of forgiveness by the blood of Christ is ours, that we become white as snow.
big: Isaiah 1:18 | mark: they shall be as white as snow

--- Joy and gladness
kicker: Psalm 51 · Make me to hear joy and gladness
big: Psalm 51:8
p: David pleads to be made to hear joy and gladness, to be cheered by the power of God's word to heal the brokenness he feels from the Father's chastening.

--- Hide thy face from my sins
kicker: Psalm 51 · Blot out all mine iniquities
big: Psalm 51:9
p: He asks God to hide His face from David's sins. To see them and remember them no more. To blot out his iniquity.
p: We know as saved Christians that this promise of God, to forgive our sins past, present and future, that He will remember them no more and place them as far from us as east is from west, is our blessed hope in the redemption that is found in Christ.

--- We take comfort
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:9
p: We take comfort in this truth when we are faithful to confess to Him;
scripture: 1 John 1:7-10 | mark: the blood of Jesus Christ his Son cleanseth us from all sin; If we confess our sins

--- Not imputed
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:9
p: This promise shows not only the faithfulness of God to forgive, but also that our sin in the flesh is not held against our ledger in Heaven. It is not imputed against those that are in Christ.
big: 1 John 3:9 | mark: he cannot sin, because he is born of God

--- Not the new man, but the flesh
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:9
p: John is not contradicting himself by saying if we say we have no sin, we are liars and simultaneously saying we cannot sin, being born of God.
p: He is describing the same feelings from the failure of the flesh David writes about here.
p: When we sin, it is not the new man, created in Christ, but the flesh.
scripture: Romans 7:15-17 | mark: it is no more I that do it, but sin that dwelleth in me

--- Nailed to the cross
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:9
p: Our flesh sins. If we deny that, we are liars. But by being born again in Christ, we do not sin. The difference is that the flesh, which we can mortify to sin, and restrain from our lusts, is dead. Nailed to the cross with Christ.
scripture: Galatians 5:24-25 | mark: have crucified the flesh

=== Restore unto me the joy
--- A right spirit
kicker: Psalm 51 · Create in me a clean heart
big: Psalm 51:10
p: Our spirit is quickened and covered by the imputed righteousness of Christ Jesus. It is His righteousness that covers our transgressions and the Spirit that seals us unto redemption.
p: So David, in his sorrow and remorse reminds God of this truth, and by doing so, reminds himself.

--- The joy of thy salvation
kicker: Psalm 51 · Restore unto me the joy
scripture: Psalm 51:11-12 | mark: Restore unto me the joy of thy salvation
p: David is not writing that he is afraid to lose his salvation, but seeking the confidence in it again that has been marred by his transgressions and the fear from the chastening he has received.
p: He is seeking to renew his relationship with God through his confession and appeals to mercy.

=== Then will I teach transgressors
--- And the purpose?
kicker: Psalm 51 · Then will I teach transgressors
p: And the purpose? Is it to selfish ends of not wanting to feel guilty and to get back on the blessings side of God? No.
big: Psalm 51:13 | mark: Then will I teach transgressors thy ways
p: It is the mercy God shows us as His children that leads us to share His praise to others, that the lost see His light upon us and they glorify Him in Heaven. It is not only the wrath that leaves the world without excuse, but the peace that passes understanding we are given by getting right with the Lord.

--- Sing aloud of thy righteousness
kicker: Psalm 51 · My tongue shall sing aloud
scripture: Psalm 51:14-15 | mark: my tongue shall sing aloud of thy righteousness
p: When He delivers us from our guilt, forgiving our sin, restoring our relationship as sons, we cannot help but sing aloud of His righteousness.

--- Joy in heaven
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:13
p: The sinner who trusts Christ is celebrated in Heaven by all the hosts of God...
big: Luke 15:7 | mark: joy shall be in heaven over one sinner that repenteth

--- The prodigal son
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:13
p: ...How much more the prodigal son who returns to his Father?
scripture: Luke 15:18-20 | mark: his father saw him, and had compassion, and ran

--- God wants to forgive us
kicker: Cross references • Psalm 51:13
p: The Father runs to meet his returning son, showering him with love. Before any confession of guilt or offer of penance or anything other than turning back towards God, the Father goes to him in compassion and restitution of their relationship.
p: God wants to forgive us more than we want to be forgiven. And we rejoice in His grace, that we may shew forth His praise.

=== A broken and a contrite heart
--- The sacrifices of God
kicker: Psalm 51 · A broken and a contrite heart
p: Again, God doesn't want sacrifice. He doesn't want our works of penance and vows to change. He takes no delight in the ritual formulas of burnt offerings, and performative acts of contrition.
big: Psalm 51:17 | mark: a broken and a contrite heart, O God, thou wilt not despise
p: It is the healing of a loving relationship that He wants for us. Even at our lowest, we should remember this, and turn to Him again.

--- Then shalt thou be pleased
kicker: Psalm 51 · The sacrifices of righteousness
scripture: Psalm 51:18-19 | mark: Then shalt thou be pleased with the sacrifices of righteousness
p: It is then, in reconciliation and peace with God, through Christ Jesus our Lord, that the works we do and the praise we sing will please Him. Then will our religion match our hearts, in healing and in service to the mighty God. Amen

